Record where IODeviceTreeAlloc stalls, step by step, on the boot-progress panel (judyp bring-up).

The 2026-11-21 14:28 photograph ends the stage history on "BC18 root nub init" with no
"BC19 root nub inited", so the boot stops inside IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(dtTop).  That
function's only substantive call is IODeviceTreeAlloc(dtTop); static review of its 623
instructions and a twin-tree DTB comparison (enchilada vs judyp: same version 17, 3265/3266
nodes, same interrupt-parent loops, no "name" property on either side) left no defensible
candidate, so the next round has to locate the step from inside the function.

Seven stage points, through the same weak boot_progress_stage() channel that produced BC13..BC18
(the kernel image carries the definition, so the calls are linked, not elided):

  BC50 IODeviceTreeAlloc entered       BC51 globals / OSSymbols ready
  BC52 root reference table built      BC53 DT child 1/1000/2000/3000 (traversal progress)
  BC54 tree traversal finished         BC55 interrupt-sharing pass finished
  BC56 IODeviceTreeAlloc returning

Reading: a stop before BC51 names the OSSymbol / zalloc_permanent_type / IOLockAlloc block; a stop
between BC52 and the first BC53 names MakeReferenceTable of the root or of the first child; a stop
inside the traversal is bounded by the BC53 interval; a stop between BC54 and BC55 names the
registry-root attach / freeDT block or the IODictionary setup; no BC56 means the interrupt pass.

Numbering starts at 50 so it cannot collide with BC10..BC22 (InitIOKit), BC30..BC33 (matching)
and BC40..BC42 (lockdown).

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IODeviceTreeSupport.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IODeviceTreeSupport.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IODeviceTreeSupport.cpp
